<Hub pillarId="taxes-and-rules">
  <p>
    This page is not tax, legal, or investment advice. Rules depend on your facts, and on the country you live in. The notes here are about US sources, because those are the ones we have read.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/filing/digital-assets">IRS digital assets page</a> says that for US tax purposes, digital assets are property, not currency. Income is taxable. If you sell, exchange, or otherwise dispose of a digital asset, you may have a gain or a loss to report, including when the result is zero. The IRS says to keep records of the purchase, the sale, the amount, and the fair market value in dollars.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brokers have a reporting form for some of this. The IRS says <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1099-da">Form 1099-DA</a> is for digital asset proceeds from broker transactions, beginning with transactions on or after January 1, 2025. A form from a broker does not replace your own records, and it does not tell you what you personally owe.
  </p>
  <p>
    “Rules” also means what the asset is not. The <a href="https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/financial-products-not-insured">FDIC</a> lists crypto assets among products that are not deposit-insured, even if a bank sold them. The <a href="https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/capital-raising-building-blocks/crypto-assets-federal-securities-laws">SEC's 2026 explainer</a> sorts crypto assets into categories, including digital commodities and digital securities, and it says the features decide which bucket applies. Bitcoin is listed there as an example of a digital commodity. That is a starting point for reading, not a personal answer about a pitch.
  </p>
  <p>
    A practical habit, not a tax plan: when you do buy, save the date, the asset, the amount, the dollar value, and the fees. The buying guide shows where that fits. <a href="/articles/how-to-buy-crypto-safely/">How to buy crypto safely for the first time</a>.
  </p>
</Hub>
